---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import GerminationSim from '../../simulators/germination/GerminationSim.astro';
import { germinationQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/germination';

const toc = [
  { id: 'seed', label: 'Inside a seed' },
  { id: 'germination', label: 'What happens during germination' },
  { id: 'conditions', label: 'The conditions seeds need' },
  { id: 'temperature', label: 'Temperature and germination speed' },
  { id: 'light', label: 'Does light matter?' },
  { id: 'experiment', label: 'Designing a fair test' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 32.2: Pollination and Fertilization (seed development and germination). OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/32-2-pollination-and-fertilization' },
  { text: 'Bewley, J. D., Bradford, K. J., Hilhorst, H. W. M., & Nonogaki, H. (2013). Seeds: Physiology of Development, Germination and Dormancy (3rd ed.). Springer.' },
  { text: 'Borthwick, H. A., Hendricks, S. B., Parker, M. W., Toole, E. H., & Toole, V. K. (1952). A reversible photoreaction controlling seed germination. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 38(8), 662–666.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="botany"
  topic="seed-germination"
  description="Interactive seed germination experiment: control water, temperature, oxygen and light for bean and lettuce seeds, and graph how many germinate each day."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={germinationQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A seed can lie dormant for years, then spring to life in a few days. What wakes it up? Set up your own petri-dish
      experiment, change the water, temperature, oxygen and light, and watch twenty seeds decide whether to germinate.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <GerminationSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>seed</strong> and set the <strong>water</strong>, <strong>temperature</strong>, <strong>air</strong> and <strong>light</strong> conditions.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong> to run the experiment for 14 days. Watch seeds swell, then grow a root and a shoot.</li>
      <li>Follow the <strong>graph</strong> of seeds germinated over time and read the <strong>conditions</strong> verdict.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Save run &amp; restart</strong>, change <strong>one</strong> condition, and run again to compare.</li>
      <li>Click a seed to see its stage. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the default conditions.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="seed">Inside a seed</h2>
  <p>
    A seed is a baby plant in a protective package. It contains an <strong>embryo</strong> (with an embryonic root, the
    radicle, and an embryonic shoot, the plumule), a <strong>food store</strong> (in the cotyledons or endosperm) and a
    tough <strong>seed coat</strong> (testa). Dry seeds are dormant: their metabolism is almost stopped, which lets them
    survive cold, drought and long journeys.
  </p>

  <h2 id="germination">What happens during germination</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Imbibition:</strong> the seed absorbs water and swells, often splitting the seed coat.</li>
    <li><strong>Activation:</strong> enzymes switch on and break down stored starch, proteins and fats into small molecules the embryo can use.</li>
    <li><strong>Respiration:</strong> the embryo respires these foods to release energy for growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergence:</strong> the radicle pushes out first, then the shoot grows toward the light. Once leaves open, the seedling can photosynthesise.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="conditions">The conditions seeds need</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Why it is needed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Water</strong></td><td>Rehydrates cells, activates enzymes and lets the seed swell</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Oxygen</strong></td><td>Needed for aerobic respiration to release energy from stored food</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Suitable temperature</strong></td><td>Enzymes work fastest at an optimum; too cold is slow, too hot denatures them</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="temperature">Temperature and germination speed</h2>
  <p>
    Every species has a <strong>base temperature</strong> below which it will not germinate, an
    <strong>optimum</strong> at which it is fastest, and a <strong>maximum</strong> above which it fails. Beans prefer
    warmth (an optimum near 25–30 °C), while lettuce prefers cool conditions and becomes dormant when it is too warm —
    which is why gardeners sow lettuce in spring and autumn.
  </p>

  <h2 id="light">Does light matter?</h2>
  <p>
    Most large seeds, like beans, germinate equally well in light or dark because they carry plenty of food. Some small
    seeds, including certain lettuce varieties, germinate much better in light. Their pigment <strong>phytochrome</strong>
    detects red light, signalling that the seed is close to the surface where the seedling will soon reach sunlight.
  </p>

  <h2 id="experiment">Designing a fair test</h2>
  <p>
    Change one <strong>independent variable</strong> at a time (for example water), keep all others the same, and
    measure the <strong>dependent variable</strong> (the percentage germinated). Use many seeds, because a few seeds are
    always dead (not viable) and individual seeds vary. Including a <strong>control</strong> dish with ideal conditions
    lets you compare results.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Germination</dt>
    <dd>The process by which a seed begins to grow into a seedling.</dd>
    <dt>Dormancy</dt>
    <dd>A resting state in which a seed does not germinate even if conditions seem suitable.</dd>
    <dt>Radicle and plumule</dt>
    <dd>The embryonic root and embryonic shoot.</dd>
    <dt>Cotyledon</dt>
    <dd>A seed leaf that often stores food for the developing seedling.</dd>
    <dt>Viability</dt>
    <dd>Whether a seed is alive and able to germinate.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Farming and gardening:</strong> sowing at the right depth, soil temperature and moisture gives even, fast crop emergence.</li>
    <li><strong>Seed testing:</strong> seed companies run germination tests to label the percentage of seeds that will grow.</li>
    <li><strong>Seed banks:</strong> facilities such as the Svalbard Global Seed Vault store seeds dry and frozen to keep them dormant for decades.</li>
    <li><strong>Brewing:</strong> malting deliberately germinates barley so its enzymes convert starch to sugar.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Seeds need soil to germinate.”</strong> Soil helps seedlings grow, but germination only needs water, oxygen and warmth.</li>
    <li><strong>“Seeds need light to germinate.”</strong> Many germinate in the dark using stored food.</li>
    <li><strong>“More water is always better.”</strong> Waterlogging starves seeds of oxygen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Germination needs water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.</li>
    <li>Stored food supplies energy until the seedling can photosynthesise.</li>
    <li>Each species has its own temperature range; some small seeds also need light.</li>
    <li>Fair tests change one variable at a time and use many seeds.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: germination times use a simple thermal-time model with natural variation; real values depend on variety and seed age.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
